{"body":"## Background\n\n[Related zulip discussion](https://radicle.zulipchat.com/#narrow/channel/369277-heartwood/topic/Anchoring.20COBs/with/585747350)\n\nLorenz Leutgeb proposed a feature he calls *anchoring* a COB: making the\ninitial operation of a COB fully deterministic so that multiple parties,\nwithout any communication, arrive at the same COB identifier when they create\na COB \"for\" the same underlying object (a commit, tree, blob, possibly a ref).\n\nToday a COB identifier is the OID of the initial commit on the COB ref. That\ncommit embeds a timestamp and an author/committer signature, so two replicas\nthat independently create \"the same\" COB end up with two distinct identifiers.\nAnchoring proposes:\n\n- zero out the commit timestamp on the initial op,\n- replace the commit author/committer with a generic identity (e.g. `radicle`),\n- require the initial op's payload to be a deterministic function of the\n  thing being anchored to,\n- optionally include the COB type name in the initial op to avoid cross-type\n  collisions.\n\nThe result: `(type, anchor)` becomes a globally agreed COB identifier with no\ncoordination.\n\n## Why this matters for `radicle-artifact`\n\nA `Release` COB is conceptually keyed by an `Oid` (commit or annotated tag).\nTwo delegates on different replicas creating a release for the same OID is the\nexpected case, not an edge case. Without anchoring, they each produce a\ndistinct COB and we reconcile after the fact. The current crate carries a\nnontrivial amount of code whose sole purpose is to handle that reconciliation.\n\n### What anchoring would let us delete\n\n- `Releases::find_or_create_by_oid` smallest-`ReleaseId` tie-break\n  (`src/lib.rs`, the loop over `find_by_oid` in `find_or_create_by_oid`).\n  With anchoring there is at most one COB per OID, so the tie-break is moot.\n- The \"union across duplicates via `find_by_cid`\" read path. Reads can target\n  a single COB instead of merging across siblings.\n- The duplicate-convergence test surface:\n  - `idempotent_create`\n  - `find_or_create_picks_smallest_id_when_duplicates_exist`\n  - `find_or_create_reuses_release_across_signers`\n\n  These collapse to a single property: \"two signers, same OID, identical COB\n  identifier.\"\n- The `Releases::create` vs. `find_or_create_by_oid` distinction itself\n  becomes uninteresting — `create` is just `find_or_create_by_oid` once the\n  identifier is a function of the OID.\n\n### What stays the same\n\n- The action model. `Action::Create { oid }` is already deterministic — no\n  timestamps, no DIDs, no signer-derived data. Subsequent ops\n  (`AddArtifact`, `AddLocation`, `Attest`, `Redact`, …) remain non-deterministic\n  per author and that is correct: those are genuine independent contributions\n  that should diverge.\n- The signer's role on subsequent ops. Anchoring only constrains the *initial*\n  op; the rest of the COB's history stays signer-attributed as today.\n\n## Open questions before adopting\n\nThese are the things this crate would want answered upstream before relying on\nanchoring:\n\n1. **Is `resource: Option<Oid>` free for non-repository OIDs?**\n   Today every caller in heartwood passes the repository ID as `resource`\n   when invoking `radicle_cob::object::collaboration::create`. The artifact\n   COB would want to pass the *anchor* OID (a commit/tag in the repo)\n   instead, or in addition. Code paths that implicitly assume\n   `resource == repo_id` — replication, authorization, indexing — need\n   auditing. Lorenz flagged exactly this as the gating concern.\n\n2. **Where does the anchor live: in `resource`, in `Create`, or both?**\n   `resource` is a single `Oid`; that fits the artifact COB cleanly. But\n   future anchored COBs may want richer anchors (a ref name, a `(repo, oid)`\n   pair across repos, a tree path). It is worth deciding now whether\n   `resource` is the anchor channel or whether `Create` grows a dedicated\n   `anchor` field, so anchored COBs do not have to migrate later.\n\n3. **Generic committer identity and signed-refs.**\n   `radicle::cob::store::Store::create` calls `repo.sign_refs(signer)` after\n   creating the COB. If the initial commit is authored by a fake identity but\n   the surrounding refs are still signed by the real signer, the trust model\n   is unchanged. Worth confirming there is no path that re-derives authority\n   from the initial commit's committer field.\n\n4. **Type-name collision avoidance.**\n   Two COB types anchored to the same OID must not produce the same COB ID.\n   Including the type name in the initial op (or in the commit message of the\n   initial commit) is the natural fix; this should be specified, not left to\n   each COB.\n\n5. **Anchoring is not always desired.**\n   For Job COBs it is unclear whether `(type, anchor)` is the right\n   uniqueness key. Two parties may legitimately want independent jobs against\n   the same anchor. Anchoring should be opt-in per COB, not a global change\n   to how `Store::create` behaves.\n\n## Suggested API shape\n\nA minimal, opt-in extension that would serve `radicle-artifact`:\n\n- `radicle_cob::object::collaboration::Create` grows an optional\n  `anchor: Option<Anchor>` (or a flag plus reuse of `resource`).\n- When `anchor` is set, `radicle_cob::object::collaboration::create`:\n  - sets the initial commit's author/committer to a fixed identity,\n  - sets author/committer timestamps to zero,\n  - includes the type name in the deterministic input.\n- `radicle::cob::store::Store::create` exposes the same option through its\n  signature so callers like `Releases::create` can request anchoring.\n\nWith that in place, `Releases::create` becomes:\n\n```rust\n// pseudocode\nlet (id, release) = store::Transaction::initial_anchored::<_, _, Transaction<R>>(\n    \"Create release\",\n    &mut self.raw,\n    signer,\n    Anchor::Commit(oid),\n    |tx, _| tx.create(oid),\n)?;\n```\n\nand `find_or_create_by_oid` reduces to a `get` followed by `create` with no\ntie-break.\n\n## Migration\n\nExisting release COBs created before anchoring would not match the\nanchored identifier for their OID. Two reasonable options:\n\n- **Cut over.** New releases use anchoring; existing ones remain reachable\n  by their current IDs and the `find_by_oid` index still finds them. The\n  `find_or_create_by_oid` tie-break code stays for one release as a\n  compatibility path, then is removed.\n- **Re-anchor on read.** When reading, if an anchored COB exists for the OID\n  prefer it; otherwise fall back to the legacy lookup. Heavier; only worth\n  it if we want the legacy COBs to \"disappear\" from the API surface.\n\nCut-over is almost certainly the right call for a young crate.","embeds":[],"id":"451b41a458b7960bea0d501ed2753c5a27c4edfd","type":"comment.edit"}